\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 In this course,\'a0
\f1\b Build an AI Agent in Python
\f0\b0 , you're building a command-line AI coding agent \'97 similar in spirit to tools like Claude Code, Aider, or Cursor's agent mode, but you're building the engine yourself from scratch in Python.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 What it actually does:
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa60\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 It's not tied to any IDE. It's a standalone Python program you run from the terminal.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 You give it a natural language prompt (e.g., "fix the bug in my calculator app").\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 The agent uses an LLM (you'll be calling Google's Gemini API) to reason about what to do.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Instead of just chatting back text, the LLM can call\'a0
\f1\b functions
\f0\b0 \'a0you've written \'97 like the ones you're building right now (
\f2 get_files_info
\f0 ,\'a0
\f2 get_file_content
\f0 ) \'97 to actually inspect a real codebase on disk.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 The agent loops: it reads files, decides what to do next, possibly calls more functions (like writing files or running Python code), and eventually reports back a result or a fix.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \strokec4 Why this lesson matters in that bigger picture:
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 The\'a0
\f2 get_file_content
\f0 \'a0function you just wrote is one of the "tools" the LLM will have access to. Think of it as giving the agent eyes \'97 the ability to peek inside a file's contents (like\'a0
\f2 calculator/main.py
\f0 ) so it can reason about what the code does. Later lessons will add functions for listing directories, writing files, and executing Python files, all scoped safely to a working directory so the LLM can't wander outside the sandboxed project.\
By the end of the course, you'll have a working CLI agent that can autonomously explore a codebase, find bugs, and even attempt fixes \'97 entirely through function-calling with an LLM, no IDE plugin required.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

